# The cspell dictionary is one alphabetically sorted word per line, so two
# branches that each add a word collide at the same line by construction — a
# conflict whose only correct resolution is "keep both sides". `merge=union`
# is git's built-in driver for exactly that: it takes both sides' lines
# instead of raising a conflict. Being built in, it needs no `merge.*.driver`
# configuration, so any clone resolves an add/add collision the same way.
#
# Union merge neither sorts nor de-duplicates, so it can leave the file out
# of order and with a word twice. Both of those are self-healing: the
# `file-contents-sorter` in cfg/pre-commit-lint.yml runs with `--unique`, so
# the next commit touching the file restores both, and neither state breaks
# anything before then — cspell tolerates them, and the dictionary-hygiene
# check reads the file into a set.
#
# One divergence is NOT self-healing: union merge keeps a line the other side
# DELETED, and an edit is a delete plus an add, so it also keeps both
# spellings of a reworded word. `--unique` cannot undo either — it only
# collapses exact duplicates. That is a live shape here, not a hypothetical,
# because `cspell-audit` drops a word that has fallen to a single file, so
# such a removal merged against a nearby addition can come back. It is
# accepted rather than fixed: a resurrected entry only over-permits one
# spelling, and the next hygiene pass re-detects it. The sorter is not what
# catches this.
#
# Do NOT extend this to a file where a dropped, doubled, or reordered line
# changes meaning. It is sound here only because the dictionary is an
# unordered set that happens to be stored sorted.
cfg/dictionary.txt merge=union
